  <h1>What To Do After a Brain Injury</h1>
  <p><em>Reviewed by our editorial team. Updated May 2026.</em></p>

  <p>The first weeks and months after a brain injury are overwhelming. There is medical urgency, family logistics, financial pressure, and a legal landscape that most families have never had reason to learn. This guide walks through what families typically need to do, in approximate priority order. It is general informational guidance, not legal or medical advice; the specifics for any particular family depend on the details of the injury, the cause, and the jurisdiction.</p>

  <h2>The first hours and days: medical priority</h2>

  <p>Acute medical care is the first priority, and nothing in the legal landscape should distract from it. Severe and moderate TBI patients are managed by neurosurgery and neurocritical care teams; mild TBI patients are typically evaluated in the emergency department and discharged or admitted briefly for observation.</p>

  <p>Things that are useful to know during the acute phase:</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>The GCS at admission and during the first 24 hours.</strong> This is the conventional severity classification and is the most important documentary fact for both medical prognosis and litigation. Ask the team what the GCS scores have been and request that they be documented clearly.</li>
    <li><strong>Whether neuroimaging has been done</strong> &mdash; CT is the acute workhorse; MRI is more sensitive to diffuse injury but is typically deferred to a stable phase.</li>
    <li><strong>Whether the patient was intubated</strong> &mdash; intubation makes the verbal GCS untestable and the recorded GCS may understate or overstate the true neurological status.</li>
    <li><strong>Whether there is a documented loss of consciousness duration and post-traumatic amnesia duration.</strong> Both are important severity indicators.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>A family member or friend should keep notes: which physicians have seen the patient, what has been done, what the team has said about prognosis, and what the next steps are. The medical record will be the eventual source, but contemporaneous notes help the family understand and remember.</p>

  <h2>The first week: stabilization and evidence preservation</h2>

  <p>As the acute medical situation stabilizes, evidence preservation becomes important for any future legal claim.</p>

  <p>Steps that matter:</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Photographs of the scene, vehicles, helmets, or equipment.</strong> If anyone took photos at the scene of the accident, gather them. If the family can return to the scene safely, additional photos are useful.</li>
    <li><strong>Police reports.</strong> Request a copy of any police accident report. These typically take a few days to a few weeks to become available.</li>
    <li><strong>Witness contact information.</strong> If witnesses were present, their contact information should be captured while memories are fresh.</li>
    <li><strong>Preserve damaged items.</strong> Do not repair or dispose of the vehicle, helmet, or other equipment involved. They may be needed for forensic examination.</li>
    <li><strong>Vehicle event-data recorder (EDR).</strong> Most modern vehicles record speed, braking, and other data in the seconds before a collision. An attorney can preserve this data through a forensic download.</li>
    <li><strong>Employer records</strong> if the injury was at work or work-related.</li>
    <li><strong>Document the medical record.</strong> The family does not need to review every page in real time, but should know that the entire chart will eventually be requested.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The first two weeks: consider an attorney consultation</h2>

  <p>If the injury was caused by another party's actions (motor vehicle accident, premises hazard, workplace incident, medical error, assault), a consultation with a catastrophic injury attorney is appropriate as soon as the family has bandwidth. Most catastrophic injury attorneys offer free initial consultations.</p>

  <p>Why early consultation matters:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>The attorney can preserve evidence that is fragile or has tight retention windows (EDR data, surveillance video, witness memories).</li>
    <li>The attorney can communicate with insurance carriers on the family's behalf, preventing recorded statements and other early-claim pitfalls.</li>
    <li>The attorney can identify all potentially liable parties (a commercial defendant produces much larger recovery than an individual; a multi-defendant case may yield more than a single-defendant case).</li>
    <li>Statute of limitations starts running at the injury date; the attorney protects the deadlines.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>Hiring an attorney early does not commit the family to anything beyond representation; almost all catastrophic injury contracts allow the family to discharge counsel later, subject to a lien for work done. The early consultation is the appropriate move even if the family is not ready to commit.</p>

  <h2>Insurance coordination</h2>

  <p>TBI care produces large bills, and insurance coordination is critical.</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Health insurance</strong> &mdash; should be the primary payer for medical care. The health insurer typically has subrogation rights against any eventual settlement (they pay the bills upfront and get reimbursed from the settlement).</li>
    <li><strong>Auto insurance (PIP / medpay)</strong> &mdash; if the injury was a motor vehicle accident, the victim's own auto insurance often has personal injury protection (PIP) or medpay coverage that pays initial medical costs regardless of fault.</li>
    <li><strong>Workers' compensation</strong> &mdash; if the injury was work-related, workers' comp pays medical and replaces wages, with statutory limits.</li>
    <li><strong>UM/UIM coverage</strong> &mdash; underinsured and uninsured motorist coverage on the victim's own auto policy may be a major recovery source.</li>
    <li><strong>Medicare and Medicaid</strong> &mdash; if applicable, with their own subrogation interests and (for Medicaid) means-testing implications.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>Do not give recorded statements to the at-fault party's insurance carrier without an attorney present. Insurance adjusters are trained to elicit statements that later limit recovery.</p>

  <h2>Neuro-rehabilitation</h2>

  <p>For moderate-to-severe TBI, inpatient rehabilitation typically follows acute care. Quality of the rehabilitation program matters enormously for outcome. The family should ask:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>Is the program a Commission on Accreditation of Rehabilitation Facilities (CARF)-accredited brain injury specialty program?</li>
    <li>What is the patient-to-therapist ratio for cognitive, speech, occupational, and physical therapy?</li>
    <li>What is the average length of stay and discharge functional level?</li>
    <li>What outpatient and community-reintegration programs follow inpatient discharge?</li>
  </ul>

  <p>Health insurance frequently pushes for shorter inpatient stays than the medical team recommends. The family may need to advocate, with attorney support if appropriate, for the medically necessary length of stay.</p>

  <h2>Neuropsychological evaluation</h2>

  <p>For any TBI beyond minor concussion, formal neuropsychological evaluation is essential. The neuropsychological battery typically includes:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>Intellectual function (estimated pre-injury IQ and current).</li>
    <li>Memory (verbal and visual; immediate, delayed, recognition).</li>
    <li>Attention and processing speed.</li>
    <li>Executive function.</li>
    <li>Language.</li>
    <li>Visuospatial function.</li>
    <li>Motor function.</li>
    <li>Mood and personality assessment.</li>
    <li>Effort and validity testing.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>Initial neuropsychological evaluation is typically done 3&ndash;6 months post-injury (when acute confusion has resolved enough to test reliably). Repeat evaluation at one year or later documents the recovery trajectory and establishes the residual baseline for life-care planning and damages.</p>

  <h2>Social Security Disability Insurance (SSDI)</h2>

  <p>TBI patients who cannot work for at least 12 months may be eligible for SSDI. The application process is slow (initial decisions in 3&ndash;5 months, denials common, appeal process can extend 12&ndash;24+ months). Filing early is appropriate. SSDI is not means-tested and is not affected by a TBI settlement.</p>

  <p>SSI (Supplemental Security Income) is means-tested and is affected by settlements; a special needs trust is typically used to preserve SSI eligibility if SSI is relevant.</p>

  <h2>Long-term care planning</h2>

  <p>For moderate-to-severe TBI patients who will need ongoing care, long-term care planning starts when the medical situation has stabilized enough to project needs.</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Life-care plan.</strong> A certified life care planner reviews the medical record and projects future medical, rehabilitation, equipment, attendant care, and supportive services year by year through expected lifespan. The life-care plan is the foundation of the future-damages claim and also provides a roadmap for family planning.</li>
    <li><strong>Special needs trust (SNT).</strong> If the patient is receiving or may need to receive needs-based public benefits (Medicaid, SSI), a special needs trust is used to hold settlement proceeds without disqualifying the patient from benefits. SNT planning should start before settlement closes.</li>
    <li><strong>Conservatorship or guardianship.</strong> If the patient lacks capacity to manage their own affairs, conservatorship (in some states, guardianship) provides legal authority for a family member or professional to make decisions. The process is jurisdiction-specific.</li>
    <li><strong>Power of attorney and advance directives.</strong> If the patient has capacity, executing or updating power of attorney and advance directives is appropriate.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Cognitive rehabilitation</h2>

  <p>Cognitive rehabilitation continues long after inpatient discharge. Components typically include:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>Neuropsychological therapy focused on attention, memory, and executive function.</li>
    <li>Speech-language therapy for language and (where relevant) swallowing.</li>
    <li>Occupational therapy for activities of daily living and (where appropriate) return to work or school.</li>
    <li>Vocational rehabilitation for patients pursuing return to work.</li>
    <li>Educational accommodation for students.</li>
    <li>Mood and behavioral therapy.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>Outcomes are best when the team is experienced in brain injury, the patient is engaged, and the program is appropriate to the patient's level. Generic outpatient therapy is rarely adequate for moderate-to-severe TBI.</p>

  <h2>Family roles</h2>

  <p>TBI is a family event, not just an individual event. Common family roles after a moderate-to-severe TBI:</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Primary caregiver</strong> &mdash; usually a spouse or parent; provides supervision, advocacy, and direct care.</li>
    <li><strong>Care coordinator</strong> &mdash; manages appointments, communications, insurance battles, and documentation.</li>
    <li><strong>Financial manager</strong> &mdash; handles bills, benefits applications, and (if applicable) the litigation and settlement process.</li>
    <li><strong>Emotional support</strong> &mdash; for the patient and for other family members affected.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>One person typically cannot do all of these. Spreading the responsibilities across family members and (where resources allow) professional case managers is sustainable; concentrating them on one caregiver typically produces burnout. Many families benefit from caregiver support groups and professional counseling.</p>

  <h2>Common pitfalls</h2>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Settling too early.</strong> Insurance carriers often offer early settlements that look generous in the acute phase but undervalue the case once the long-term prognosis is clear. Settling before the medical situation has stabilized typically loses recovery.</li>
    <li><strong>Signing releases for the at-fault carrier.</strong> Do not sign anything from the at-fault insurance carrier without attorney review.</li>
    <li><strong>Recorded statements.</strong> Decline recorded statements to insurance adjusters until attorney representation is in place.</li>
    <li><strong>Posting on social media.</strong> Insurance carriers monitor social media to find content that contradicts injury claims. Limit posting or restrict privacy settings.</li>
    <li><strong>Missing the statute of limitations.</strong> Each state has its own deadline. An attorney protects deadlines.</li>
  </ul>

